/*
Q)  What is Set Interface?

  Part of java.util
  Extends Collection
  Does NOT allow duplicates
  Order depends on implementation
  Uses equals() + hashCode()

✔ Set doesn’t allow duplicates
✔ Uses equals() & hashCode()
✔ HashSet → fastest
✔ TreeSet → sorted
✔ LinkedHashSet → order preserved

  public interface Set<E> extends Collection<E>

*/

    Set<Integer> set = new Set<>();

1️⃣ INSERTION IN SET
   // add(E e) - hashCode() is calculated
                 Bucket index is found
                 equals() is checked
                 If unique → inserted
                 If duplicate → ignored
         Return Value :
                 true → inserted
                 false → duplicate

     set.add(10);


// addAll(Collection c)
   set.addAll(list);

   Working:
      Adds only unique elements
      Duplicates automatically skipped

 2️⃣ DELETION IN SET

 // remove(Object o)
    set.remove(10);

    Step-by-step:
       Finds bucket using hashCode
       Compares using equals()
       Removes element if found

 // removeAll(Collection c)
    set.removeAll(otherSet);

    Removes all matching elements

 // retainAll(Collection c)
    set.retainAll(otherSet);

    Keeps common elements
    Deletes remaining

 // Method: clear()
 set.clear();

 Removes all elements
 Set becomes empty

3️⃣ TRAVERSAL IN SET
🔸 Using Iterator (MOST COMMON)
Iterator<Integer> it = set.iterator();
while(it.hasNext()){
    System.out.println(it.next());
}

✔ Safe
✔ Fail-fast

🔸 Using Enhanced for-loop
for(Integer i : set){
    System.out.println(i);
}

✔ Simple
❌ No removal allowed

🔸 Using forEach (Java 8)
set.forEach(e -> System.out.println(e));

4️⃣ SEARCH / CHECK OPERATIONS
🔸 Method: contains(Object o)
set.contains(20);
✔ Returns true / false

🔸 Method: containsAll(Collection c)
set.containsAll(list);

5️⃣ SIZE & STATUS METHODS
🔸 size()
set.size();
Number of elements

🔸 isEmpty()
set.isEmpty();

6️⃣ CONVERSION METHODS
🔸 toArray()
Object[] arr = set.toArray();

🔸 toArray(T[] a)
Integer[] arr = set.toArray(new Integer[0]);

7️⃣ SET IMPLEMENTATION–WISE BEHAVIOR
🔹 HashSet
Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

No order
One null allowed
Fastest

🔹 LinkedHashSet
Set<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();

Maintains insertion order
One null allowed

🔹 TreeSet
Set<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();

Sorted order
No null allowed
Uses Red-Black Tree

8️⃣ COMPLETE EXAMPLE (Insertion, Deletion, Traversal)
Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

// insertion
set.add(10);
set.add(20);
set.add(30);
set.add(20); // ignored

// deletion
set.remove(10);

// traversal
for(Integer i : set){
    System.out.println(i);
}

// size
System.out.println("Size: " + set.size());

✔ Set doesn’t allow duplicates
✔ Uses equals() & hashCode()
✔ HashSet → fastest
✔ TreeSet → sorted
✔ LinkedHashSet → order preserved